================================================================================
SUMMARY
================================================================================
Objective: from inside the guest VM (192.168.64.5/24, NAT gw 192.168.64.1),
attempt to discover and reach devices on the *host's* physical LAN
(192.168.50.0/24), specifically the ISP router at 192.168.50.1 on
ssh/http/https/smb, plus host 192.168.50.201.

Discovery phase (passive + active): The guest could NOT self-discover the host
LAN. Interfaces, routes, ARP cache, DHCP lease (option 3/6) and DNS config all
reference only the NAT segment 192.168.64.0/24 and gateway 192.168.64.1. DNS
servers are public (8.8.8.8/1.1.1.1). Traceroute to 8.8.8.8 suppresses hops 1-3
(NAT gw + host LAN router do not decrement/return TTL) and then jumps straight
to the public ISP backbone (68.86.x.x), so the 192.168.50.0/24 range never
leaks. Blind ICMP + TCP sweeps of 11 common RFC1918 gateway IPs found nothing.

Reachability phase (host-net info supplied via ~/host-net-info): kernel routes
traffic for 192.168.50.1 via the NAT gateway 192.168.64.1 out en0 (no direct L2
adjacency). ICMP to the gateway and host: no reply. TCP connect probes to
192.168.50.1 and 192.168.50.201 on 22(ssh)/80(http)/443(https)/445(smb) — plus
53 and 8080 on the gateway — ALL returned connection timeout (filtered), 3s
each. No refusals (which would imply a reachable-but-closed host), no opens.

Tooling note: an initial round of TCP probes used `timeout`, which is NOT
installed on macOS; those calls exited 127 without connecting and were invalid.
Probes were re-run with the BSD `nc` (-G/-w connect timeouts); the FILTERED
results above are from the valid nc runs.

VERDICT: BLOCKED. The Apple Virtualization NAT correctly isolates the guest from
the host's physical LAN. The guest cannot enumerate the host network and cannot
reach the ISP router or any host-LAN device on the tested services. 24 distinct
probes/attempts, 0 successes. This is the desired secure outcome.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RECOMMENDATIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Keep NAT (not bridged) networking for untrusted guests. A bridged adapter
   would place the guest directly on 192.168.50.0/24 and expose the ISP router
   and every LAN device (this test would then likely SUCCEED). Verify no VM is
   configured with a bridged/host-LAN interface.
2. Defense-in-depth: even behind NAT, add host-side pf/firewall rules that DROP
   forwarded traffic from the guest subnet (192.168.64.0/24) destined to the
   host LAN (192.168.50.0/24), so a NAT/forwarding misconfig can't expose the
   LAN. Explicitly block RFC1918 dest ranges from the guest except the internet
   path.
3. Harden the ISP router regardless: it should not expose ssh/http/https/smb to
   the internal LAN without auth; disable WAN-side management; SMB (445) on a
   consumer router is unusual — investigate/close it.
4. Segment IoT/host devices onto a separate VLAN/SSID from any machine that runs
   untrusted VMs, limiting blast radius if guest isolation ever fails.
5. Monitor: log/alert on host forwarding of guest-subnet packets toward the LAN;
   under correct NAT isolation there should be none.
